1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:10,200
Global positioning satellites, bioengineered tomatoes, ultra-sensitive yield monitors, and food processing plants on wheels.

2
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:15,860
From the plow to precision farming, it's no longer Old MacDonald's Farm.

3
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:20,160
Next up, farming technology on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,560
The Johansson Farm in central Kansas.

5
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:44,300
Greg Johansson starts the day by feeding his family.

6
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:48,260
But his job is to feed the world.

7
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:56,100
American agriculture, from seed to shelf, is so efficient, most people don't think much about it.

8
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:59,340
But growing food is a sophisticated business.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,080
employing science, computer-guided ground-shaking machinery,

10
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:08,700
and sometimes controversial techniques.

11
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:16,100
American farmers generate $200 billion of produce annually,

12
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:20,660
providing the U.S. with the most plentiful food basket

13
00:01:20,661 --> 00:01:23,420
and least expensive food bill in the world.

14
00:01:23,421 --> 00:01:31,560
Well, certainly having a safe and affordable and abundant food supply is critical to the

15
00:01:31,561 --> 00:01:33,638
national security of this country.

16
00:01:33,639 --> 00:01:37,060
We've never had to go to war for a shortage of food like

17
00:01:37,061 --> 00:01:39,101
most countries of the world have.

18
00:01:39,102 --> 00:01:42,440
So it gives our country kind of a leg up that we are a

19
00:01:42,441 --> 00:01:43,840
food-sufficient society.

20
00:01:43,841 --> 00:01:48,980
The farmer's job is complex.

21
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:56,660
Feed the world, protect the environment, provide inexpensive food, stay in business.

22
00:02:00,740 --> 00:02:03,580
Greg manages 3,000 acres.

23
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:09,680
His hours depend on the needs of the land and the crops it produces.

24
00:02:09,681 --> 00:02:15,560
You know, it's basically your typical farm operation in this part of the country.

25
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:20,180
Wheat, milo, beans, alfalfa, and beef cattle.

26
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:26,100
Like the cycle of life of the crops that he grows,

27
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:32,700
Greg is engaged in a never-ending repetition of preparation, planting, nurturing, and harvesting.

28
00:02:32,701 --> 00:02:39,700
In this program, we'll see just what Greg's yearly tasks are like.

29
00:02:39,701 --> 00:02:47,700
Greg starts by using a tractor and field conditioner to prepare his land for planting.

30
00:02:47,701 --> 00:02:54,700
The Johansson family's rich farming heritage dates back many generations.

31
00:02:54,701 --> 00:02:59,700
But the origins of farming date back 10,000 years.

32
00:02:59,701 --> 00:03:05,660
Agriculture allowed for everything.

33
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:11,700
You couldn't have urban populations, you couldn't have stable populations without agriculture.

34
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:17,460
Before that, people were chasing after animals, they were moving around a lot, or they were foraging.

35
00:03:18,460 --> 00:03:23,840
Ancient agronomists learned to break up the soil with digging sticks in preparation for seeding.

36
00:03:23,841 --> 00:03:32,600
Around 3500 BC, Egyptians created a wooden wedge-shaped implement, tipped with an iron

37
00:03:32,601 --> 00:03:36,220
share or blade to cut a trough in the earth.

38
00:03:36,221 --> 00:03:40,580
They hitched their plow to oxen.

39
00:03:40,581 --> 00:03:46,240
In the 11th century, Europeans added a moldboard behind the share to turn the soil over once

40
00:03:46,241 --> 00:03:47,240
it was broken.

41
00:03:47,241 --> 00:03:49,573
The plow does several things.

42
00:03:49,574 --> 00:03:54,240
First of all, it turns under crop residue, it opens up the

43
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:59,640
soil, and thirdly, it's used for weed control.

44
00:03:59,641 --> 00:04:06,640
In the newly established United States, people expanded westward, displaced natives, and

45
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:08,769
established homesteads.

46
00:04:08,770 --> 00:04:14,000
The rich soil in the Midwest prompted a design change of the ancient

47
00:04:14,001 --> 00:04:15,628
ancient implement.

48
00:04:15,629 --> 00:04:21,960
The cast iron plows popular for the sandy soil back east just couldn't

49
00:04:21,961 --> 00:04:22,661
cut it.

50
00:04:22,662 --> 00:04:30,160
The problem was that the soil would stick to the plow and they had to stop every

51
00:04:30,161 --> 00:04:38,955
so often and clean the dirt off of the moldboard before they could proceed.

52
00:04:38,956 --> 00:04:41,300
In 1837, John Deere,

53
00:04:41,301 --> 00:04:46,640
an Illinois blacksmith, found inspiration for a better plow when visiting a local sawmill.

54
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:52,600
He noticed a broken steel saw blade that was highly polished from use.

55
00:04:54,660 --> 00:04:57,895
Deer took the saw blade and fashioned it into a plow.

56
00:04:57,896 --> 00:05:00,520
When finished, he went around demonstrating

57
00:05:00,521 --> 00:05:02,707
his plow's amazing properties.

58
00:05:02,708 --> 00:05:07,300
As it was pulled through the ground, the steel would polish and

59
00:05:07,301 --> 00:05:11,200
become very slick and that sticky soil wouldn't stick to it.

60
00:05:11,201 --> 00:05:13,280
Well this was quite a revolution

61
00:05:13,281 --> 00:05:17,000
in the development of the plow in that particular part of the country.

62
00:05:18,580 --> 00:05:23,197
By 1855 John Deere was selling 13,000 plows a year.

63
00:05:23,198 --> 00:05:27,180
The John Deere Agricultural Empire was born.

64
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:33,467
In the mid-1800s 55% of Americans lived on farms.

65
00:05:33,468 --> 00:05:36,560
Farming was a back-breaking enterprise.

66
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:38,460
even with the aid of horses.

67
00:05:40,260 --> 00:05:43,460
But a new invention would begin to change that.

68
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:50,040
The tractor was probably the most significant agricultural innovation of the 20th century.

69
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:58,900
The tractor allowed farmers to get rid of approximately 23 million draft animals, horses and mules.

70
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:04,520
To feed these animals required approximately 80 million acres of cropland

71
00:06:04,521 --> 00:06:07,400
and another 80 million acres of pasture land.

72
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:14,100
The first traction engines, or tractors, were steam-powered.

73
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:18,820
These monstrous machines were built like locomotives,

74
00:06:19,740 --> 00:06:23,440
burning coal, wood, or straw to produce steam that drove the wheels.

75
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:29,380
They consumed enormous amounts of water and fuel,

76
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:31,780
but produced relatively little power.

77
00:06:31,781 --> 00:06:38,180
For the most part, steam tractors stayed on the sidelines and ran stationary devices.

78
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:46,380
But the role of the tractor began to change with the advent of the gasoline engine in the late 1800s.

79
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,600
The company, founded on polished steel plows, expanded its product line.

80
00:06:53,780 --> 00:07:00,880
John Deere purchased the Waterloo Gasoline Traction Company in Waterloo, Iowa in 1918.

81
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:03,840
And that was a tremendous move.

82
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:11,100
They saw the future, of course, was going to be tractors powered by gasoline engines instead of horsepower.

83
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:16,020
Weighing tons less, yet having comparable horsepower,

84
00:07:16,021 --> 00:07:19,720
the gasoline tractor snuffed out the fire-breathing steam engines

85
00:07:19,721 --> 00:07:23,100
and put a lot of animals out to pasture.

86
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:30,040
In the 1920s and 30s, there were organized campaigns, quite substantial campaigns,

87
00:07:30,041 --> 00:07:34,660
trying to defend the horse and delay or stop the spread of the tractors.

88
00:07:34,760 --> 00:07:37,980
There was an organization called the Horse Association of America.

89
00:07:39,060 --> 00:07:42,300
One of the things the Horse Association of America did was they set up a lot of tests

90
00:07:42,301 --> 00:07:47,020
with tractors and horses to show that horses were more efficient.

91
00:07:47,021 --> 00:07:50,740
And at least in some cases where the temperature was very hot,

92
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:55,280
they overworked their horses and the animals didn't survive the test.

93
00:08:00,040 --> 00:08:03,800
Tractors meant larger farms and fewer farmers.

94
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:11,020
Tractors and plows shredded the prairie, helping to feed an increasing metropolitan population.

95
00:08:12,580 --> 00:08:19,280
But the combination of tractor, plow, and drought led to the Dust Bowl of the 1930s.

96
00:08:20,820 --> 00:08:25,660
The plow broke the virgin sod and tight root structure that protected the underlying soil.

97
00:08:25,661 --> 00:08:35,840
The soil was now susceptible to the fierce prairie wind, leading to one of the greatest

98
00:08:35,841 --> 00:08:41,320
ecological disasters in history.

99
00:08:41,321 --> 00:08:49,360
Clouds of dust overtook 150,000 square miles of Texas, Oklahoma, Colorado, New Mexico and

100
00:08:49,361 --> 00:08:50,360
Kansas.

101
00:08:50,361 --> 00:08:57,780
The dirty 30s, the first time I remember, don't know what's coming up, but it sure is black.

102
00:08:58,780 --> 00:09:00,080
It was dust.

103
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:06,740
In response, farmers looked for alternative implements that didn't leave the soil as vulnerable.

104
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:13,680
New farming techniques, conservation efforts, and rain helped settle the dust.

105
00:09:13,681 --> 00:09:23,280
In the 20th century, the tractor became the cornerstone of farming.

106
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:27,080
Manufacturers introduced improvements.

107
00:09:28,520 --> 00:09:30,820
Hydraulics to raise and lower machinery.

108
00:09:31,860 --> 00:09:33,980
Power takeoffs to run equipment.

109
00:09:34,980 --> 00:09:37,520
And the luxury of a cab.

110
00:09:37,521 --> 00:09:46,520
The Soundguard body was an integral part of the tractor which enclosed the operator.

111
00:09:46,521 --> 00:09:54,520
It kept the noise out, all fully air conditioned, tremendous visibility around the tractor.

112
00:09:54,521 --> 00:10:00,520
It really focused on operator comfort and convenience.

113
00:10:00,521 --> 00:10:09,060
Costing more than $200,000, today's largest tractors run 425-horsepower diesel motors.

114
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:11,220
Some even have tracks.

115
00:10:13,700 --> 00:10:15,000
Behemoth four-wheel drive.

116
00:10:15,540 --> 00:10:17,160
Nearly every comfort of home.

117
00:10:18,300 --> 00:10:24,080
Since farmers can spend 14 hours a day, six days a week on the tractor, it is home.

118
00:10:25,240 --> 00:10:27,040
People think it's boring when you're on a tractor.

119
00:10:27,220 --> 00:10:29,840
I mean, they think, well, you don't have nobody to talk to.

120
00:10:30,520 --> 00:10:32,320
When I get on a tractor, it's just peaceful.

121
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:35,580
And I'm in there thinking about what you could do maybe next year.

122
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:40,460
Greg Johanson's ground is conditioned and ready for seeding.

123
00:10:40,980 --> 00:10:43,040
His next task will be planting.

124
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:47,000
Coming up, seed.

125
00:10:47,580 --> 00:10:52,500
As technologically amazing as the equipment, but a great deal more controversial.

126
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,720
There are close to 1 billion acres of farmland in the United States.

127
00:10:59,340 --> 00:11:02,040
Farming technology will return on Modern Marvels.

128
00:11:05,800 --> 00:11:11,400
Greg Johanson manages the family farm with his two brothers, Mike and Tom.

129
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:18,660
After tilling the land, it's planting season, and Greg readies his equipment to sow wheat.

130
00:11:22,260 --> 00:11:25,160
Greg's grain drill opens a small trench in the earth,

131
00:11:25,161 --> 00:11:30,120
drops seed at precise increments and at exact depths,

132
00:11:30,960 --> 00:11:32,780
then covers the seed with dirt.

133
00:11:35,700 --> 00:11:39,980
Gregg's drill is just the latest incarnation of planting machinery and methods.

134
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:45,300
By comparison to the plow, the planter is a relatively new invention.

135
00:11:46,040 --> 00:11:51,840
Up into the 1800s, the planting of corn, for instance, was still very labor-intensive.

136
00:11:51,841 --> 00:11:56,120
It involved an individual farmer carving a hole into the field with his hoe

137
00:11:56,121 --> 00:12:00,960
and then dropping individual seeds into those holes and recovering them.

138
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:08,780
In the early 1700s, Jethro Tull, an English lawyer,

139
00:12:09,260 --> 00:12:11,380
invented a mechanical seed drill for planting.

140
00:12:13,780 --> 00:12:18,900
His device planted in neat rows for easy weeding and at a uniform depth,

141
00:12:18,901 --> 00:12:22,280
which Tull discovered was important to the health of the plant.

142
00:12:25,940 --> 00:12:29,700
Inventions like Tull's revolutionized the way people grew crops

143
00:12:29,701 --> 00:12:31,060
and cared for the ground.

144
00:12:31,920 --> 00:12:35,160
That environment-farmer partnership endures today.

145
00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:41,060
You've got to take care of your land if the land's going to take care of you.

146
00:12:41,840 --> 00:12:43,060
That's the way I look at it.

147
00:12:44,920 --> 00:12:47,540
Greg's father, Robert, retired years ago.

148
00:12:48,180 --> 00:12:50,700
But like many aging farmers, he can't slow down.

149
00:12:51,900 --> 00:12:54,680
He's seen more than 50 years of change.

150
00:12:55,360 --> 00:13:00,540
They've bred better wheat, we've got better seed wheat, and you've got fertilizer.

151
00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:05,800
Technological innovation hasn't been restricted to machinery.

152
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:12,880
Arguably, the biggest change in agriculture came with the increased use of chemical fertilizer in the mid-20th century.

153
00:13:12,881 --> 00:13:22,360
As the crop grows, nutrients are depleted from the soil and must be replenished for a healthy crop next year.

154
00:13:24,320 --> 00:13:26,680
The concept is a very old one.

155
00:13:30,900 --> 00:13:33,440
Ancient Chinese used animal manure.

156
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:37,640
The Romans used bird manure and garbage compost.

157
00:13:37,641 --> 00:13:44,480
Native Americans taught the pilgrims how to grow maize by burying a fish beside the planted seeds.

158
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:56,180
In 1842, an English agriculturalist, John Laws, produced phosphate fertilizer by breaking down animal bones with sulfuric acid.

159
00:13:58,680 --> 00:14:06,140
After World War I, companies producing synthetic nitrate for explosives shifted to making nitrogen fertilizer.

160
00:14:07,640 --> 00:14:14,540
In the 1950s, companies introduced inexpensive fertilizers which ignited a green revolution.

161
00:14:16,740 --> 00:14:18,460
Yields skyrocketed.

162
00:14:19,300 --> 00:14:26,060
I used some fertilizer on my own ground and had a little left, and so I had some rented ground.

163
00:14:26,340 --> 00:14:30,680
And the landlord wasn't too enthused about using it, you know, paying for it.

164
00:14:30,681 --> 00:14:35,960
So I just finished what I had left and made an X out there in the field

165
00:14:35,961 --> 00:14:40,720
and didn't say nothing to him until the following year.

166
00:14:40,840 --> 00:14:44,180
And when we got ready to cut a wheat, why, he asked what that was.

167
00:14:45,080 --> 00:14:47,420
And you could see it from quite a little ways off,

168
00:14:47,460 --> 00:14:49,040
and when you went through it with the combine,

169
00:14:49,041 --> 00:14:53,220
you could sure see more grain coming in the bin as he was going through that X.

170
00:14:53,940 --> 00:14:57,380
After that, why, he was all enthused about using fertilizer.

171
00:14:57,381 --> 00:15:02,500
In the decades that followed the introduction of inexpensive fertilizers, yields tripled.

172
00:15:03,960 --> 00:15:06,780
And more food has historically been the goal of agriculture.

173
00:15:08,180 --> 00:15:11,480
But fertilizer wasn't the only reason for higher yields.

174
00:15:12,700 --> 00:15:13,680
Seed changed.

175
00:15:17,240 --> 00:15:21,620
Hybrid corn introduced in the 1920s produced bigger bounties.

176
00:15:22,820 --> 00:15:25,680
The ancestor of corn is called teosinti.

177
00:15:25,681 --> 00:15:33,260
This is what corn was before our foremothers got a hold of it 10, 15,000 years ago.

178
00:15:33,740 --> 00:15:40,080
And over, you know, 15 millennia of artificial selection, they turned this into this.

179
00:15:41,300 --> 00:15:47,940
In today's biotechnological world, scientists can alter plant characteristics almost overnight.

180
00:15:48,860 --> 00:15:55,420
Genetically modified, or GM crops, result from a technology that is as controversial as it is powerful.

181
00:15:55,680 --> 00:16:00,680
Scientists now move specific genes from one organism into another.

182
00:16:00,681 --> 00:16:05,680
Some of the genes that we're putting into plants come from organisms that are not plants,

183
00:16:05,681 --> 00:16:10,680
come from animals or bacteria. Why is that possible? Why is that not unnatural?

184
00:16:10,681 --> 00:16:14,680
Well, it's possible because DNA, nucleic acids, are the language of life,

185
00:16:14,681 --> 00:16:17,680
and that's a virtually universal language.

186
00:16:17,681 --> 00:16:22,680
Every organism on the planet uses these compounds to encode their hereditary characteristics.

187
00:16:22,681 --> 00:16:27,050
characteristics. There's nothing in a gene which says, I belong in a strawberry.

188
00:16:27,051 --> 00:16:27,760
I belong in a

189
00:16:27,761 --> 00:16:32,016
fish. If found in a corn plant, you know, please return to owner.

190
00:16:32,017 --> 00:16:33,980
I mean, the living world isn't

191
00:16:33,981 --> 00:16:34,810
like that.

192
00:16:34,811 --> 00:16:41,620
Proponents of the new technology promise higher yielding crops, decreased reliance

193
00:16:41,621 --> 00:16:47,200
on chemical pesticides and herbicides, and plants with higher nutritional values.

194
00:16:47,201 --> 00:16:54,740
it's not a substitute for working on poverty and the other issues that that are causing people to

195
00:16:54,741 --> 00:17:00,580
be malnourished but if you have only a limited budget and you have to eat a certain type of

196
00:17:00,581 --> 00:17:06,300
commodity and we can modify it in such a way that has higher nutrition it seems really

197
00:17:06,301 --> 00:17:13,960
virtually immoral not to do it i mean why not do that seed companies swear to the benefits and

198
00:17:13,961 --> 00:17:19,912
safety of GM food. But opponents call them frankenfoods.

199
00:17:19,913 --> 00:17:23,100
Concerned groups believe there

200
00:17:23,101 --> 00:17:28,240
could be unforeseen health risks, including the accidental introduction of unknown allergens

201
00:17:28,241 --> 00:17:29,795
into common food.

202
00:17:29,796 --> 00:17:36,840
The development of regulations and the widespread use of genetically modified

203
00:17:36,841 --> 00:17:43,080
crops is going ahead at a rate much faster than our ability to assess the risks associated with it.

204
00:17:46,903 --> 00:17:57,240
In fact, there's very little good risk assessment that has been done in terms of understanding some of the ecological consequences of the material that is already very

205
00:17:57,241 --> 00:18:03,120
widely grown, never mind the next generations that are getting ready to be put into production.

206
00:18:03,121 --> 00:18:12,340
Of course we have to be sure they're safe, we have to test that, but they're certainly as safe as the genetic engineering we've been doing for the last hundred years.

207
00:18:13,080 --> 00:18:24,560
The debate regarding genetically modified foods may be heated, but with more than 45 GM crops on the market, chances are you're consuming them right now.

208
00:18:27,820 --> 00:18:31,180
Back at the farm, Greg Johansson's crop is growing.

209
00:18:32,140 --> 00:18:34,280
It's the most vulnerable time of the year.

210
00:18:35,280 --> 00:18:38,340
A host of assailants could wipe out his hard work.

211
00:18:38,341 --> 00:18:43,867
35% of corn and 55% of soybeans produced in the U.S.

212
00:18:43,868 --> 00:18:47,480
in 1999 were genetically modified.

213
00:18:48,440 --> 00:18:51,420
Farming technology will return on Modern Marvels.

214
00:18:53,200 --> 00:18:56,280
Greg Johanson has completed planting his crops.

215
00:18:57,180 --> 00:18:59,360
But he doesn't sit back and wait for harvest.

216
00:19:00,120 --> 00:19:05,640
As the crops grow, Greg's battling insects, weeds, and even the weather.

217
00:19:06,640 --> 00:19:10,380
You can have a perfect year growing and you think you've got it made,

218
00:19:11,220 --> 00:19:13,760
and in an hour time a hailstorm's got you completely gone.

219
00:19:13,960 --> 00:19:15,120
You know, it's completely gone.

220
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:21,660
There's no business that is more prone to natural disaster than agriculture.

221
00:19:22,320 --> 00:19:25,300
I mean, you know, just imagine if you ran a business outside

222
00:19:25,301 --> 00:19:30,620
with items that you were producing that could be destroyed overnight.

223
00:19:30,621 --> 00:19:34,020
It could be a flood, a hurricane, a tornado, a hailstorm, or a drought.

224
00:19:35,640 --> 00:19:39,140
If the weather doesn't get Greg's crops, insects might.

225
00:19:40,420 --> 00:19:41,120
Pests...

226
00:19:41,121 --> 00:19:44,760
Some of the chemicals that they dealt with were really toxic.

227
00:19:45,040 --> 00:19:47,720
You know, they were using arsenic pretty freely on trees.

228
00:19:48,280 --> 00:19:50,700
I always like to be out there spraying arsenic around,

229
00:19:50,701 --> 00:19:53,800
and all you have to protect yourself is a little handkerchief over your mouth.

230
00:19:55,120 --> 00:19:57,060
In the mid-20th century,

231
00:19:57,720 --> 00:20:01,800
scientists created extremely effective chemical herbicides to kill weeds

232
00:20:01,801 --> 00:20:04,200
and pesticides to kill insects.

233
00:20:05,060 --> 00:20:05,920
Yields jumped.

234
00:20:10,480 --> 00:20:14,120
One of the most infamous pesticides is DDT.

235
00:20:15,560 --> 00:20:21,180
A Swiss chemist, Paul Mueller, discovered DDT's insect-killing properties in 1939.

236
00:20:23,560 --> 00:20:29,140
DDT was extensively used during World War II by both the civilian and military population

237
00:20:29,141 --> 00:20:33,020
to control lice, mosquitoes, and other insects.

238
00:20:34,200 --> 00:20:41,200
Malaria cases dropped from 75 million incidents a year to 5 million in less than a decade.

239
00:20:44,200 --> 00:20:49,200
It was so effective, farmers started using DDT after the war.

240
00:20:50,200 --> 00:20:57,200
Farmers in the U.S. used 1.2 billion pounds of DDT between 1950 and 1972.

241
00:20:57,201 --> 00:21:07,200
In 1948, Dr. Mueller won a Nobel Prize for his work.

242
00:21:07,201 --> 00:21:15,200
The benefits of DDT were obvious, but the properties that made DDT work so well as a

243
00:21:15,201 --> 00:21:20,200
pesticide also made it an environmental poison.

244
00:21:20,201 --> 00:21:26,200
DDT is easily absorbed and is slow to break down in the soil.

245
00:21:26,201 --> 00:21:35,200
In 1962, Rachel Carson's book Silent Spring reported DDT killed fish and birds.

246
00:21:35,201 --> 00:21:38,200
Other studies linked DDT to cancer.

247
00:21:38,201 --> 00:21:43,200
Her bestseller prompted a White House report calling for new federal regulation.

248
00:21:43,201 --> 00:21:50,200
Finally, in 1972, the U.S. government banned DDT.

249
00:21:50,201 --> 00:21:56,700
But the overuse of other chemicals produced similar negative effects.

250
00:21:56,701 --> 00:22:02,780
I think we're in trouble. We really are. And sometimes I think we need just to back

251
00:22:02,781 --> 00:22:09,760
up a little bit, take it a little bit slower, remember what our parents taught us, and not

252
00:22:09,761 --> 00:22:14,040
go overboard on this pesticide bit.

253
00:22:14,041 --> 00:22:19,840
Today, most farmers hire licensed professionals to apply the pesticide and herbicide products

254
00:22:19,841 --> 00:22:25,840
that regulatory agencies deem safe.

255
00:22:25,841 --> 00:22:31,220
Ironically, the same companies that make pesticides and herbicides are creating genetically modified

256
00:22:31,221 --> 00:22:37,260
crops that require less chemical.

257
00:22:37,261 --> 00:22:42,400
Seed companies introduced corn that generates its own pesticide.

258
00:22:42,401 --> 00:22:49,000
Derived from a soil bacterium, Bacillus thuringiensis, Bt corn cells are toxic to the corn borer

259
00:22:49,001 --> 00:22:50,180
pests that eat it.

260
00:22:52,620 --> 00:22:55,660
You no longer have to spray to eliminate that insect.

261
00:22:56,300 --> 00:23:00,340
And most of those insecticides are the ones that we really don't want to fool with.

262
00:23:00,700 --> 00:23:02,820
We don't want the residues in the crop.

263
00:23:02,821 --> 00:23:05,440
It's a boon to me, and it's a boon to the consumer.

264
00:23:07,300 --> 00:23:12,380
Seed companies also promote herbicide-resistant soybeans that require less herbicide.

265
00:23:13,520 --> 00:23:16,520
But like other GM issues, there's debate.

266
00:23:16,521 --> 00:23:21,740
There is a great number of unanswered questions here.

267
00:23:21,820 --> 00:23:24,060
This technology has not been tested.

268
00:23:25,520 --> 00:23:30,740
Irregardless of what FDA and EPA have put a rubber stamp on,

269
00:23:30,820 --> 00:23:36,060
they used, for the most part, information that the genetic engineering companies supplied to them.

270
00:23:37,340 --> 00:23:42,320
Environmental watchdogs worry about insects developing resistance to genetic pesticides,

271
00:23:42,321 --> 00:23:44,960
killing non-target insects,

272
00:23:45,600 --> 00:23:48,960
or cross-pollination between GM plants and weeds,

273
00:23:49,780 --> 00:23:52,840
producing uncontrollable herbicide-resistant weeds.

274
00:23:55,020 --> 00:23:59,600
Furthermore, there's concern about gene patents granted to the seed companies.

275
00:24:00,520 --> 00:24:05,500
I think biotechnology can provide a way to produce crops with less water,

276
00:24:05,740 --> 00:24:07,640
less energy, less pesticides.

277
00:24:07,641 --> 00:24:11,820
But I also think we've got to do a very good job of regulating this

278
00:24:11,821 --> 00:24:14,580
to protect public health and the environment as well,

279
00:24:14,720 --> 00:24:16,140
and I think you can do both.

280
00:24:17,920 --> 00:24:21,160
An alternative to GM food is organic.

281
00:24:22,740 --> 00:24:25,960
Organic foods are non-genetically modified crops

282
00:24:25,961 --> 00:24:29,660
grown without chemical fertilizer, pesticide, or herbicide.

283
00:24:31,100 --> 00:24:34,760
The demand for organic foods has doubled in the last 10 years.

284
00:24:37,300 --> 00:24:39,980
The University of California Santa Cruz

285
00:24:39,981 --> 00:24:43,340
runs one of the oldest organic research departments in the U.S.

286
00:24:44,860 --> 00:24:47,960
Here, researchers develop non-chemical techniques

287
00:24:47,961 --> 00:24:50,720
to help farmers grow crops and control pests.

288
00:24:51,920 --> 00:24:55,140
A lot of it is about managing the farm habitat

289
00:24:55,141 --> 00:24:57,720
to encourage beneficial insects

290
00:24:57,721 --> 00:25:01,080
that will suppress the populations of pests.

291
00:25:01,660 --> 00:25:06,060
And that may involve planting particular crops next to each other.

292
00:25:06,061 --> 00:25:10,460
It may involve planting particular vegetation around field margins.

293
00:25:11,440 --> 00:25:16,100
I think as a society, we have to think about how to encourage farming practices

294
00:25:16,101 --> 00:25:18,720
that don't cause environmental problems

295
00:25:18,721 --> 00:25:24,320
and find a way of encouraging farmers to use those, even if it costs more,

296
00:25:25,700 --> 00:25:30,140
so that we don't, as a society, then face the costs of cleaning up the problems later.

297
00:25:30,141 --> 00:25:37,140
Applying that research is Mark Marino.

298
00:25:37,141 --> 00:25:39,220
Mark works for Earthbound Farm.

299
00:25:39,221 --> 00:25:44,140
Their 10,000 acres represent one of the largest organic farms in the U.S.

300
00:25:44,141 --> 00:25:49,900
Without the aid of chemicals, organic farming requires a lot more attention and manpower

301
00:25:49,901 --> 00:25:52,680
than conventional farming.

302
00:25:52,681 --> 00:25:58,720
Mark applies 20 to 30 tons of compost per acre every year to fertilize the earth.

303
00:25:58,721 --> 00:26:00,950
Plants are like people.

304
00:26:00,951 --> 00:26:06,960
If you have a good diet, you will not be as susceptible to flu

305
00:26:06,961 --> 00:26:10,273
bugs or cold germs. And plants are very much like that.

306
00:26:10,274 --> 00:26:12,260
If you can feed the soil and make

307
00:26:12,261 --> 00:26:17,440
a smorgasbord of really nice nutrients in the soil that the plant can take in, then

308
00:26:17,441 --> 00:26:21,536
they will be healthy and they will not have the tendency to be sick.

309
00:26:21,537 --> 00:26:22,680
And that's what the

310
00:26:22,681 --> 00:26:25,640
bugs attack, the sick ones. It's survival of the fittest.

311
00:26:25,641 --> 00:26:30,560
organic products make up about one percent of the market

312
00:26:30,561 --> 00:26:37,540
prices tend to be higher but many consumers are willing to pay for their personal peace of mind

313
00:26:37,541 --> 00:26:44,640
even if it tastes better and seems safer some say organic farming can't produce the

314
00:26:44,641 --> 00:26:52,520
inexpensive food an expanding world requires although banned in the united states approximately

315
00:26:52,521 --> 00:26:56,558
two dozen countries continue to use DDT to control malaria.

316
00:26:56,559 --> 00:26:58,680
Farming technology will return.

317
00:27:07,340 --> 00:27:14,020
The Johansson brothers prepare the combine, a giant machine used for harvesting grain.

318
00:27:15,400 --> 00:27:18,540
They're fortunate. It looks like a bumper crop.

319
00:27:18,541 --> 00:27:21,620
Harvest is my one paycheck for the whole year.

320
00:27:22,040 --> 00:27:25,640
I mean, you've worked all year long to put it in, to watch over it,

321
00:27:25,840 --> 00:27:29,040
and pray over it that the weather doesn't take it from you.

322
00:27:29,500 --> 00:27:32,500
And the end result is what you have in the bin.

323
00:27:36,280 --> 00:27:40,360
Grain farmers in the Midwest rely on big equipment for the job.

324
00:27:41,760 --> 00:27:47,320
This $150,000 combine is a food processing plant on wheels,

325
00:27:47,321 --> 00:27:51,700
cutting, threshing and cleaning grain in one slick operation.

326
00:27:55,280 --> 00:28:01,740
The machine devours 36-foot swaths of grain, reeling it in and shearing the stocks.

327
00:28:03,560 --> 00:28:09,140
The cut grain enters the bowels of the machine, where beater heads pound the seed from the stock.

328
00:28:11,620 --> 00:28:14,340
Vibrating sieves send grain to a holding bin.

329
00:28:14,341 --> 00:28:17,460
while refuse has passed out the back.

330
00:28:20,140 --> 00:28:22,760
The operator rides in the cab of the combine,

331
00:28:23,340 --> 00:28:27,660
where a host of sensors tell him everything from shaft speed to bin volume.

332
00:28:29,580 --> 00:28:33,700
Driving a combine in Harvest, it's probably the most important job out there.

333
00:28:34,880 --> 00:28:38,600
You've got to watch how high you're cutting so you're not missing anything.

334
00:28:39,320 --> 00:28:42,400
You're taking anywhere from 20 to 30 foot swath.

335
00:28:42,401 --> 00:28:48,960
If your field's not flat, if you've got terraces, you're constantly trying to keep one end out of the ground.

336
00:28:49,660 --> 00:28:52,180
It's pretty hairy at times.

337
00:28:53,620 --> 00:28:57,280
The modern combine didn't appear until the early 20th century.

338
00:28:58,300 --> 00:29:03,720
Before that, the three functions, cutting, threshing, and cleaning, were done with separate devices.

339
00:29:05,380 --> 00:29:07,620
Reaping is an activity that's been around since antiquity.

340
00:29:07,621 --> 00:29:13,000
Early farmers used flint-bladed knives to cut the stalks of grain.

341
00:29:13,380 --> 00:29:17,180
They evolved eventually into a forward-angled sickle,

342
00:29:17,400 --> 00:29:21,440
which was used to separate the stalk from the head,

343
00:29:22,060 --> 00:29:24,700
and eventually evolved into the scythe.

344
00:29:25,800 --> 00:29:28,460
The scythe was a blade and long handle.

345
00:29:29,720 --> 00:29:34,260
Eventually, farmers attached cradles to the scythes to catch wheat, increasing productivity.

346
00:29:37,620 --> 00:29:42,100
A person came behind the scythe and manually bound wheat into sheaves for threshing.

347
00:29:44,100 --> 00:29:48,720
The back-breaking work had remained basically the same for thousands of years.

348
00:29:50,840 --> 00:29:57,080
But the farmer got a break when Cyrus McCormick introduced his mechanical reaper in the 1830s.

349
00:29:57,540 --> 00:30:01,500
The horse-drawn device mowed down wheat using a reciprocating saw.

350
00:30:02,420 --> 00:30:07,560
A man manually raked the wheat off the back where it would be gathered and sheaved by another person.

351
00:30:07,620 --> 00:30:18,420
Mechanization revolutionized harvesting, but McCormick's device only cut the wheat.

352
00:30:18,421 --> 00:30:23,520
The next step involved separating the grain from the straw.

353
00:30:23,521 --> 00:30:31,020
Farmers in the mid-1800s started using a mechanical thresher that reduced labor even more.

354
00:30:31,021 --> 00:30:36,020
The thresher had a series of rotating teeth that stripped the seed from the stalk.

355
00:30:36,021 --> 00:30:42,540
And J.I. Case was really instrumental in the development of the threshing machine.

356
00:30:43,440 --> 00:30:48,600
This thresher initially was powered by human power, in other words, by turning a crank.

357
00:30:49,320 --> 00:30:54,380
But one of the things that Case developed was the utilization of animal power.

358
00:30:55,200 --> 00:30:58,720
Animals, horses on a treadmill or oxen on a treadmill.

359
00:31:01,100 --> 00:31:04,760
The Case company continued improving threshing technology.

360
00:31:06,020 --> 00:31:09,880
Steam-powered threshing machines were prevalent into the 1930s.

361
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:16,940
Men fed cut wheat into the thresher, which mechanically beat the grain free from the straw.

362
00:31:19,420 --> 00:31:26,360
Sieves and a fan separated grain from chaff, expelling straw out one end and grain out the other.

363
00:31:29,620 --> 00:31:35,420
Farming took one more gigantic leap forward with the invention of the combined harvester thresher.

364
00:31:36,020 --> 00:31:38,960
a machine capable of both cutting and separating.

365
00:31:40,940 --> 00:31:43,120
Combines saved a heck of a lot of work.

366
00:31:43,320 --> 00:31:44,400
A heck of a lot of work.

367
00:31:45,180 --> 00:31:49,700
Because when you're using a steam engine and a thrashing machine,

368
00:31:50,440 --> 00:31:52,920
there's about 11 people with that thrashing machine,

369
00:31:53,220 --> 00:31:54,360
so there'd be quite a crew around.

370
00:31:56,760 --> 00:32:00,360
The first combines in the late 1800s were horse-drawn.

371
00:32:01,260 --> 00:32:04,420
It took an enormous amount of power to pull the complicated mechanisms.

372
00:32:04,421 --> 00:32:11,420
All of the cutting, threshing and separating functions depended on gearing attached to the wheels.

373
00:32:14,420 --> 00:32:16,420
Forty animals might be used.

374
00:32:18,420 --> 00:32:21,420
Tractor power made things easier in the 1900s.

375
00:32:22,420 --> 00:32:27,420
And around 1910, manufacturers introduced self-propelled combines.

376
00:32:27,421 --> 00:32:34,420
A crew of 10 to 15 people was replaced by one man and one machine.

377
00:32:34,421 --> 00:32:40,420
The combine had an effect on the farm similar to the tractor.

378
00:32:40,421 --> 00:32:45,420
Farm size grew while farm population shrank.

379
00:32:45,421 --> 00:32:50,420
More people moved off the farm and into the city.

380
00:32:50,421 --> 00:32:56,420
In the 20th century, combines became more comfortable, sophisticated and productive.

381
00:32:56,421 --> 00:33:02,420
We have combines today that are just absolutely magnificent machines.

382
00:33:02,421 --> 00:33:06,420
They're factories on wheels that run through the field harvesting

383
00:33:06,421 --> 00:33:13,420
troil rows of corn at a time or have a 36-foot platform to direct cut wheat or grain.

384
00:33:13,421 --> 00:33:18,420
A lot of these combines move across North America with custom harvesters

385
00:33:18,421 --> 00:33:22,420
or contract harvesters that harvest the crops.

386
00:33:22,421 --> 00:33:29,560
Custom cutters are wandering bands of men and machines who spend as much as nine months

387
00:33:29,561 --> 00:33:32,800
a year cutting grain for hire.

388
00:33:32,801 --> 00:33:37,040
Following the progression of harvest from Texas north into Canada.

389
00:33:37,041 --> 00:33:43,900
The allure of the nomadic part of it wears off pretty soon with the grind of what's it

390
00:33:43,901 --> 00:33:50,740
like not to be at home, what's it like to live in a travel trailer.

391
00:33:50,741 --> 00:33:57,740
The average crew maintains two to six combines with matching grain trucks and trailers.

392
00:33:57,860 --> 00:34:04,860
An outfit might have more than one million dollars of equipment.

393
00:34:04,861 --> 00:34:11,060
Custom cutters swoop into an area, cut for a few days, then move north into a new region.

394
00:34:11,061 --> 00:34:17,140
In a flash, the grains cut and they're ready to go.

395
00:34:18,577 --> 00:34:23,787
Now you load this combine on a trailer. The combine weighs over 20,000 pounds.

396
00:34:23,788 --> 00:34:28,128
The truck weighs 20,000 pounds. The trailer weighs 10,000 pounds.

397
00:34:28,129 --> 00:34:33,671
The tires on the combine, even after you've taken the header off, are 14 feet wide.

398
00:34:33,672 --> 00:34:38,480
Just a mass of kinetic energy just waiting for an accident, if you will.

399
00:34:38,481 --> 00:34:43,720
Most farmers own a combine and cut their own grain.

400
00:34:45,300 --> 00:34:48,320
The machinery is expensive and commodity prices are low.

401
00:34:49,380 --> 00:34:54,760
For every dollar spent at the grocery store, the farmer only gets 10 to 20 cents.

402
00:34:57,380 --> 00:35:00,400
Farmers have to manage more acreage to make a profit,

403
00:35:00,840 --> 00:35:04,680
and smaller farmers are finding it harder and harder to survive.

404
00:35:04,681 --> 00:35:10,720
You have a bumper crop and then you turn around and look at the price of the crop

405
00:35:10,721 --> 00:35:14,080
and you're wondering how you're going to make it to the next one.

406
00:35:15,120 --> 00:35:21,300
And that's the big letdown is knowing that you don't have any more money than you had a year ago.

407
00:35:21,940 --> 00:35:24,580
You're hopefully stretching enough to pay the bills.

408
00:35:27,280 --> 00:35:30,760
It's been a good yet exhausting year on the Johansson farm.

409
00:35:32,080 --> 00:35:33,380
But Greg can't rest.

410
00:35:34,380 --> 00:35:38,000
He's already back in the tractor preparing for the coming planting season

411
00:35:38,001 --> 00:35:40,040
and looking forward to next year.

412
00:35:41,440 --> 00:35:44,600
Every year, equipment and technology change.

413
00:35:45,800 --> 00:35:49,040
Greg's neighbor already has the latest global positioning system

414
00:35:49,041 --> 00:35:50,840
and computer yield monitor.

415
00:35:51,780 --> 00:35:55,080
Greg knows to survive, he'll have to invest as well.

416
00:35:56,660 --> 00:36:01,460
Coming up, preparing for next season as Future Tech hits the farm.

417
00:36:03,380 --> 00:36:07,420
In 1900, 41% of the U.S. population were farmers.

418
00:36:08,120 --> 00:36:10,320
Today, 2% farm the land.

419
00:36:11,120 --> 00:36:12,820
Farming technology will return.

420
00:36:14,980 --> 00:36:17,780
The Johansson brothers are finishing harvest.

421
00:36:18,920 --> 00:36:21,860
They're taking the final loads of wheat to the grain elevator.

422
00:36:24,800 --> 00:36:32,180
Rising from the flat prairie landscape stand tall white storage structures that can hold 200 million pounds of grain.

423
00:36:33,380 --> 00:36:40,380
Grain is unloaded and stored until food companies buy it to make bread, crackers or pizza crust.

424
00:36:41,380 --> 00:36:47,380
Greg sells his wheat and keeps track of income versus operating expenses on a computer.

425
00:36:48,380 --> 00:36:53,380
Like the rest of the world, farming has undergone a technological revolution in the last 20 years.

426
00:36:56,380 --> 00:37:00,380
Greg's neighbor, Lee Scheifler, is on the forefront of technology.

427
00:37:00,381 --> 00:37:06,266
technology. He's equipped his machinery with global positioning receivers.

428
00:37:06,267 --> 00:37:07,380
His GPS guided

429
00:37:08,300 --> 00:37:14,080
sprayer tells him exactly where he's been and what he has left to cover. This helps

430
00:37:14,081 --> 00:37:17,469
him avoid over spraying and wasting chemicals.

431
00:37:17,470 --> 00:37:21,080
GPS is the technology behind a new way of farming

432
00:37:21,081 --> 00:37:27,200
technology behind a new way of farming called site-specific or precision agriculture.

433
00:37:30,700 --> 00:37:36,080
Precision agriculture treats the field not as one unit but as many smaller units with

434
00:37:36,081 --> 00:37:42,240
different needs. By taking soil samples a farmer gets an electronic map of the

435
00:37:42,241 --> 00:37:48,680
nutrient requirements of the field as they vary within the field. This is a

436
00:37:48,681 --> 00:37:54,400
prescription specifically for that field that tells you that the northwest corner gets more

437
00:37:54,401 --> 00:37:58,212
fertilizer than the southwest corner. So the rate is continually changing.

438
00:37:58,213 --> 00:37:59,500
The application rate will

439
00:37:59,501 --> 00:38:05,694
be changing automatically based on this map, this computer, and GPS.

440
00:38:05,695 --> 00:38:08,700
Lee also uses GPS during harvest.

441
00:38:09,680 --> 00:38:15,200
His yield monitor, which tells how much grain he's cutting, is compared against his GPS location.

442
00:38:16,180 --> 00:38:19,460
The yield monitor is the report card, if you will.

443
00:38:20,460 --> 00:38:24,320
How did this field perform, not on a whole field basis,

444
00:38:24,440 --> 00:38:27,780
which we gather by simply weighing everything that went off the field,

445
00:38:28,140 --> 00:38:31,080
but how did it perform in different areas of the field?

446
00:38:31,400 --> 00:38:34,560
I always knew there was variability until I actually saw that measured.

447
00:38:34,940 --> 00:38:36,180
I didn't realize how much.

448
00:38:38,760 --> 00:38:41,140
If the tractor has a map of a field,

449
00:38:41,500 --> 00:38:44,380
and with GPS knows where it is within the field,

450
00:38:45,000 --> 00:38:46,440
Why can't the tractor run itself?

451
00:38:48,260 --> 00:38:51,020
Trimble navigation is making that a reality.

452
00:38:53,200 --> 00:38:58,800
The autopilot system allows for satellite-navigated control of the tractors going across the field.

453
00:38:59,240 --> 00:39:05,520
It takes control of the steering and the hydraulics so that the operator has a hands-off operation

454
00:39:05,521 --> 00:39:08,540
where the guidance just takes over and runs the tractor by itself.

455
00:39:09,140 --> 00:39:11,480
This frees the operator up to monitor the equipment,

456
00:39:11,481 --> 00:39:14,880
reduces stress and makes it fun to operate that tractor in the cab.

457
00:39:16,760 --> 00:39:19,940
The Trimble system is accurate down to an inch.

458
00:39:20,720 --> 00:39:24,660
But a person can't just point the tractor in the direction of the field and let it go.

459
00:39:25,400 --> 00:39:29,760
The sophisticated system is designed to help the operator drive straight lines.

460
00:39:30,620 --> 00:39:36,120
That first line then is set between the A point at the beginning, the B point as the finish.

461
00:39:36,540 --> 00:39:40,040
Then each subsequent pass will be parallel to that first line.

462
00:39:41,480 --> 00:39:48,480
Benefits to driving straight lines would include the operator's ability to drive even at night.

463
00:39:48,481 --> 00:39:55,480
Chemicals are more efficiently applied because there are no gaps or double coverage between passes.

464
00:39:55,481 --> 00:40:02,480
Every technological innovation is helping farmers produce more food.

465
00:40:02,481 --> 00:40:03,181
food.

466
00:40:03,182 --> 00:40:12,500
What's easy to forget is that we live in a very unusual age in which Americans, for the most

467
00:40:12,501 --> 00:40:16,550
part, do not worry about starvation on their doorstep.

468
00:40:16,551 --> 00:40:19,100
You don't have to go back very far

469
00:40:19,101 --> 00:40:24,340
in history in which most people were worried about where they were going to get enough to eat,

470
00:40:24,341 --> 00:40:30,820
and famines were a regular fact of life in now-developed countries like Western Europe.

471
00:40:30,821 --> 00:40:37,820
And it's also easy to forget that much of the world hasn't passed that threshold.

472
00:40:37,821 --> 00:40:44,820
We've seen all this technological change in the last 150 years, and particularly the last 50 years.

473
00:40:44,821 --> 00:40:47,585
I guess my view is that it's not going to stop.

474
00:40:47,586 --> 00:40:49,820
If anything, it's going to accelerate.

475
00:40:49,821 --> 00:40:53,820
This means that productivity is going to continue going up.

476
00:40:53,821 --> 00:40:56,820
It means that there will be fewer farmers.

477
00:40:56,821 --> 00:41:02,420
Farmers have to compete with non-farmers to keep their sons on the farm.

478
00:41:02,421 --> 00:41:08,260
And if they don't, it means the sons go off and become engineers and technicians and university professors.

479
00:41:08,820 --> 00:41:10,880
I just think that's a losing battle.

480
00:41:15,500 --> 00:41:21,540
After college, Greg was the only Johansson brother who came back to work full-time on the farm.

481
00:41:22,880 --> 00:41:25,520
The farm couldn't support three families.

482
00:41:26,820 --> 00:41:31,000
Low farm prices led the others to pursue alternative careers.

483
00:41:33,620 --> 00:41:37,300
I didn't have enough for all three boys to each have a farm.

484
00:41:37,600 --> 00:41:40,440
I'd like to have seen every one of them being able to farm on his own,

485
00:41:40,520 --> 00:41:42,320
but there's just no way that it'll work.

486
00:41:45,140 --> 00:41:47,940
And whether it'll work after I'm gone, I don't know.

487
00:41:48,080 --> 00:41:48,920
That's up to them.

488
00:41:52,220 --> 00:41:55,680
Mike works evenings, weekends, and vacations on the farm.

489
00:41:56,820 --> 00:41:59,940
To him, the land is more than just a business venture.

490
00:42:01,300 --> 00:42:07,100
You know, it's hard to put into words how I feel about the farm and the ground, you know, the land.

491
00:42:07,420 --> 00:42:12,000
I'm not a real emotional person, and I have a hard time expressing those kind of things.

492
00:42:12,001 --> 00:42:12,900
You can ask my wife.

493
00:42:14,240 --> 00:42:18,620
But, you know, they're not making any more of it.

494
00:42:18,940 --> 00:42:20,080
The land that's here is here.

495
00:42:23,300 --> 00:42:25,740
It's just, I don't know.

496
00:42:25,741 --> 00:42:32,740
It's just something that I enjoy doing. It's pretty neat.

497
00:42:34,740 --> 00:42:41,740
So for the time being, the Johansson family and others like them continue to farm.

498
00:42:41,741 --> 00:42:45,740
Racing to keep abreast of the latest technology.

499
00:42:45,741 --> 00:42:48,740
Working long hours in the dirt.

500
00:42:48,741 --> 00:42:53,740
Caring about what they do and feeding the world.

